---
import Layout from '../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
---

<Layout
        title="Implementation notes"
        description="How this Jobber-style clone is organized and how to extend it."
        pubDate={new Date('January 01 2025')}
        heroImage="/blog-placeholder-about.jpg"
>
        <p>
                This project reimagines the Jobber experience with a focus on clarity and extensibility. The
                landing page highlights the core flows operators care about: quoting, scheduling, dispatching,
                invoicing, and client communication. Everything is intentionally minimal so you can layer in your
                brand, components, or integrations without fighting the starter.
        </p>

        <p>
                Under the hood you'll find a simple Astro layout that renders feature blocks, implementation
                steps, and clear calls-to-action. Content is written in plain HTML so you can swap copy, wire in
                forms, or connect CMS-driven sections as you scale. The grid-based layout adapts to different
                screen sizes with no additional dependencies.
        </p>

        <p>
                Want to extend this clone? Add service-specific pricing tables, embed booking widgets that honor
                crew availability, and connect payment providers to turn accepted quotes into instant deposits.
                Because the structure is lightweight, you can also translate the copy or retheme the accent
                color without reworking the underlying markup.
        </p>

        <p>
                The roadmap for this starter includes: richer component examples for scheduling views, sample
                automation timelines for reminders and follow-ups, and integration stubs for accounting tools.
                Contributions are welcome—treat this as a sandbox to prototype the operational muscle your crews
                need to win more jobs.
        </p>
</Layout>
